#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119925 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119499 :question/id #uuid "3843dc13-0908-5d4c-adfe-836a928a1410" :question/math-academy-id "e-10908" :question/problem "Consider the following vectors:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a}_1 = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 0\\end{array} \\right]}, \\qquad\n\\mathbf{a}_2 = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}- 1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 6\\end{array} \\right]}, \\qquad\n\\mathbf{a}_3 = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}2 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 1\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$\n\nAssume that $x_1,x_2,$ and $x_3$ are real numbers. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The equation $x_1\\mathbf{a}_1+x_2\\mathbf{a}_2+x_3\\mathbf{a}_3=\\mathbf{0}$ has an infinite number of nonzero solutions.\n\nII. The vectors $\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2,$ and $\\mathbf{a}_3$ are linearly independent.\n\nIII. The vectors $\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2,$ and $\\mathbf{a}_3$ are linearly dependent." :question/worked-solution "We say that three vectors $\\mathbf{v}_1, \\mathbf{v}_2,$ and $\\mathbf{v}_3$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\nx_1 \\mathbf{v}_1 + x_2 \\mathbf{v}_2 + x_3 \\mathbf{v}_3 = \\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nis $x_1 = x_2 = x_3 = 0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\nThe equation $x_1\\mathbf{a}_1+x_2\\mathbf{a}_2+x_3\\mathbf{a}_3=\\mathbf{0}$ corresponds to the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ 4{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2}+5{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ 6{x}_{2}+{x}_{3} &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTo solve the system, we create its augmented matrix and reduce it using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}2 & -1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 4 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 1 & 0\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}2 & -1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 1 & 0\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-1){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}2 & -1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the matrix above, we conclude that the basic variables are $x_1$ and $x_2,$ while $x_3$ is the free variable. \n\nSince there is one free variable, the equation $x_1\\mathbf{a}_1+x_2\\mathbf{a}_2+x_3\\mathbf{a}_3=\\mathbf{0}$ has an infinite number of nonzero solutions, which means that the vectors are linearly dependent. \n\nTherefore, statements II is false, while statements I and III are true."} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "52a45814-d1e2-5948-b7bd-69dcaf6cd0eb" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049058 :topic/difficulty 0.9585750007816604 :topic/encompasses [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186428063 :encompassing/rationale "Initial Codex estimate from captured target-band crossing frequency. Selected account foundations: 1 rises / 2 eligible passed source activities = 0.500000; nearest selected fraction = 1/2. Crossing activity numbers [2]; waits [2]. Separate account estimates: differential=1 (1/1); foundations=1/2 (1/2). Selection: most observations, then lower weight, then account name; no averaging. Single-activity estimates included. Intervening target updates: True. Crossing frequency is the chosen practical estimate of encompassing coverage; a multi-band rise counts once. This does not interpret color steps as physical repetition units. Method: encompassing-report.md. Evidence: reference/mathacademy/encompassing-analysis-2026-10-11/frequency-fractions/foundations-frequency-estimates.json, pair 1048->1047. All account evidence retained in the prepared import's selected-relationships.json." :encompassing/topic {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050267} :encompassing/weight 0.5} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186428064 :encompassing/rationale "Initial Codex estimate from captured target-band crossing frequency. Selected account foundations: 1 rises / 1 eligible passed source activities = 1.000000; nearest selected fraction = 1. Crossing activity number